ashed box highlights the top-left plot (DAE, Style Space). A black curved arrow points from this highlighted area to the right side of the image, accompanied by the text '(DAE shows a clearer separation than VAE in Style Space)'. Below the document snippet, the text 'Evidence Yelpr in the document' is visible. Above the document, the query is stated: 'Question: Which model's style space shows a clearer separation between different styles according to Figure 2?'.\n\nThe right side of the image lists the responses from four different AI models to the question, each marked with an emoji indicating correctness or error. \n1. 'GPT-4o-mini' is marked with a red 'X' emoji. Its response states: 'According to Figure 2, the VAE model's style space shows a clearer separation between different styles.'\n2. 'LightRAG' is marked with a red 'X' emoji. Its response states: 'According to Figure 2, the Variational Autoencoder (VAE) shows a clearer separation between different styles in its style space compared to the Deterministic Autoencoder (DAE).'\n3. 'MMGraphRAG' is marked with a red 'X' emoji. Its response states: 'According to Figure 2, the model's style space shows a clearer separation between different styles in the Variational Autoencoder (VAE) compared to the Deterministic Autoencoder (DAE).'\n4. 'RAG-Anything' is marked with a green checkmark emoji and the label '(Correct)'. Its response states: 'The DAE model's style space shows a clearer separation between different styles according to Figure 2.'\n\nThe visual layout contrasts the incorrect answers of the baseline models (GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG), which all incorrectly identify the VAE model, with the correct answer provided by RAG-Anything, which correctly identifies the DAE model based on the visual evidence in the highlighted figure."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > A.2 ADDITIONAL CASE STUDIES\n- Neighbor Text: • Demonstrating Visual Reasoning Capabilities. Figure 5 illustrates how RAG-Anything handles complex visual reasoning tasks involving chart interpretation. The query asks which GCAN submodel component removal yields the lowest accuracy on Twitter15. Traditional approaches struggle with spatial relationships between visual elements. RAG-Anything addresses this challenge by constructing a structured graph representation of the bar plot. Bars, axis labels, and legends become interconnected nodes. These are linked by semantic relations such as bar-of and label-applies-to. This graph-based approach enables precise alignment between visual and textual elements. The system correctly identifies the bar labeled \"-S-A\" (removing source tweet embeddings and dual co-attention) and its corresponding accuracy value as the lowest performer. Baseline methods that flatten visual information often misinterpret spatial relationships. They frequently conflate nearby components. RAG-Anything’s structured representation preserves critical visual-textual associations. This leads to accurate query resolution and proper attribution of performance drops to \"-S-A\". • Handling Complex Tabular Structures. Figure 6 showcases RAG-Anything’s ability to navigate intricate tabular data where structural disambiguation is crucial. The query seeks the model combination achieving the highest AUPRC value for the Evidence Inference dataset—a task complicated by repeated row labels across multiple datasets within the same table. This scenario highlights a fundamental limitation of conventional approaches that struggle with structural ambiguity in data. RAG-Anything overcomes this by parsing the table into a comprehensive relational graph where headers and data cells become nodes connected through explicit row-of and column-of relationships. This structured representation enables the system to correctly isolate the Evidence Inference dataset context and identify \"GloVe + LSTM – Attention\" with a score of 0.506 as the optimal configuration. By explicitly preserving hierarchical table constraints that other methods often collapse or misinterpret, RAG-Anything ensures reliable reasoning across complex multi-dataset tabular structures. A.3 CONTEXT-AWARE MULTIMODAL PROMPTING\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/2eff967c30113700d0d143e34cf7a7c676d98aaf9b2778005df8904bceaf69d3.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 6: Tabular navigation case. RAG-Anything locates the highest AUPRC value (0.506), while the compared approaches struggle with structural ambiguity.\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image presents a comparative case study, labeled as Figure 6, illustrating the performance of different Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems in navigating complex tabular data. The layout is divided into two main sections: a visual input on the left and textual outputs on the right.\n\nOn the left, a 'Multimodal Document' is depicted, showing a page with a table titled 'Evidence table in the document'. A specific section of this table is highlighted with a blue dashed box and annotated with the text '(Table with spatial ambiguity)'. A black arrow points from this highlighted table section to the top of the right-hand panel, indicating the source of the query. The query itself is stated at the top left: 'Question: Which model combination for the Evidence Inference dataset has the highest AUPRC value?'.\n\nThe right side displays the responses from four different systems, arranged in a two-column format. Each response is preceded by the system's name and an icon.\n\n1.  **GPT-4o-mini**: Located in the top-left of the response panel. The text reads: 'The model combination for the Evidence Inference dataset with the highest AUPRC value is the BERT-to-BERT model, achieving an AUPRC of 0.455.'\n2.  **LightRAG**: Located in the top-right. The text reads: 'The model combination \"BERT + LSTM - Attention\" has the highest AUPRC value for the Evidence Inference dataset at 0.429.'\n3.  **MMGraphRAG**: Located in the bottom-left. The text reads: 'The model combination for the Evidence Inference dataset with the highest AUPRC value is the \"Bert-To-Bert\" model, which achieved an AUPRC score of 0.708.'\n4.  **RAG-Anything (Correct)**: Located in the bottom-right. The label 'Correct' is highlighted in green. The text reads: 'The model combination for the Evidence Inference dataset with the highest AUPRC value is GloVe + LSTM - Attention, achieving a value of 0.506.'\n\nThe visual comparison demonstrates that while GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, and MMGraphRAG provide incorrect values or model combinations, RAG-Anything correctly identifies 'GloVe + LSTM - Attention' with a value of 0.506 as the answer, successfully resolving the structural ambiguity present in the source table."}
